/*  modSeamT -- THE MODIFIER SEAM. IT IS CLOSED, AND IT WAS A SPELLING FAULT.

    Commissioned by Tony, SEQ 192, 2026-09-22. Re-rooted SEQ 193, same day, on
    Tony's Xcode walk. The question was whether a term's MODIFIER survives
    generation. It does. What did not survive was the SPELLING the fixture was
    first written in, and the fixture was measuring that instead.

    ⚠⚠ THE TWO SPELLING FAULTS, RULED BY TONY 2026-09-22 (SEQ 193).
    A bare `"a"-` in an isRule rule STEALS `GrouP` as its tag, and the tag is
    then promoted to a rule -- so the member is both label and rule and is NOT
    grammar's GrouP. That is where `optT = CodE { return GrouP() && GrouP(); }`
    came from: two DIFFERENT literals emitting the SAME call, neither carrying
    its own text. And the MODIFIER BELONGS ON THE LABEL, not on the value:
    `one="a"-` refuses, `one-="a"` and `two?-="b"` do not.
    ⚠ THERE IS NO generateParse MODIFIER-DROP DEFECT. Recorded beside
    generateParse in `incant/designDocs`.

    THE ROOT, as Tony walked it:
        optT isRule one-="a" two?-="b" ;
    and the body it generates, verbatim, on this binary:
        optT = CodE {
            return one() && two();
            }
    The labels ARE the terms now, and they carry their own data -- dumpContents
    reads `one=a string` and `two=b string`.

    ⚠⚠ THE `?` IS ASSERTED BY A PAIR OF DRIVES, NOT BY READING THE BODY. The
    modifier is nowhere in the emitted text: `two()` is spelled identically
    whether two is optional or mandatory, because the modifier lives in two's
    own rStuff min/max and the call site never mentions it. So the only honest
    assertion is behavioural, and it takes two drives:

        optT("a")   optional ABSENT   -- must WIN, and `two` must NOT fire
        optT("ab")  optional PRESENT  -- must WIN, `two` MUST fire, consumed 2

    ⚠ THE FIRST ROW IS THE ONE THAT READS THE `?`. A rule that stopped
    processing the optional -- min left at 1 -- would FAIL optT("a"). The
    second row is its anti-vacuity sibling: a rule that dropped `two` from the
    chain ENTIRELY would pass the first row and leave `b` uneaten in the second.
    Neither row alone certifies anything; the pair does.
    ⚠ AND THE PAIR IS ALSO THE ZERO/NON-ZERO PAIR THE FLEET RULES ASK FOR --
    "two did not fire" is a zero-expecting row and cannot stand alone.

    THE CURSOR IS STILL THE SECOND INSTRUMENT AND STILL NOT THE VERDICT. Both
    roads report WIN on every cell here; the verdict alone separates nothing.
    `MARKARM drive base=` is the zero point, `MARKPT 2b-before-pop` is the last
    instant the drive's own cursor exists, and CONSUMED IS AN OFFSET, `mark -
    base`, NEVER AN ADDRESS -- the addresses move every run under ASLR and a
    pinned one would be rule H3's exact prohibition. `in=` carries the other
    half: DRIVE-STRING means the mark is still inside the drive, not-in-drive
    means it left it entirely, which is what a fully-consumed 1-char drive does.

    ⚠⚠ MS-3's BORN-RED ROW IS RETIRED, AND IT IS A SUBJECT CHANGE RATHER THAN A
    ROW THAT STARTED PASSING. The 2026-09-22 pin read `NEW road consumed 0, want
    1` and was measured on the BARE-LITERAL root, which the ruling above names
    as a spelling fault -- so the thing it was pinned against no longer exists.
    Under the labelled root the two roads AGREE completely, cell for cell:
        optT("a")    OLD  WIN, two silent, in=not-in-drive
        optT("a")    NEW  WIN, two silent, in=not-in-drive
        optT("ab")   OLD  WIN, two fired,  in=DRIVE-STRING consumed 2
        optT("ab")   NEW  WIN, two fired,  in=DRIVE-STRING consumed 2
    That agreement is the seam being closed. It is NOT a re-pin to silence a
    red: the old row's root is gone from this file.

    ⚠⚠ MS-5 IS TERMINAL AND EVERYTHING BELOW IT IS UNREACHABLE. `repT("aaa")`
    EXITS 139 -- `GroupItem::getText()` on a null `this`, GroupItem.mm:1322,
    reached through `ACTFIRE fireLabelMethod GrouP`. MEASURED PRE-EXISTING at
    b5e1557, the seal before the attachLabel conversion, where it exits 139 too
    with optT's cells reading identically. `"a"+-` (noLabel) does NOT crash; the
    LABELLED repetition does. It is NOT the modifier seam and it carries its own
    row, `docs/fixIts.md` F-103, so it is not re-attributed here.
    ⚠ The sentinel and MS-6/MS-7 are pinned UNREACHABLE rather than pinned to a
    value: a row behind a crash asserts nothing, and pinning it to its absent
    reading would be an absence check wearing a diff's clothes.
    ⚠ AND THE OLD-ROAD DRIVES MUST COME FIRST OR THEY ARE NOT THE OLD ROAD --
    `parser()` is a one-way door -- so repT's new-road reading CANNOT live here
    beside its old-road drive. It is measured in `tester`, which Tony steps.

    ROWS
      MS-5   repT OLD    exit 139                                pinned defect
      MS-1   optT("a")  OLD  WIN, two silent, not-in-drive       the `?` row
      MS-1b  optT("ab") OLD  WIN, two fired,  consumed 2         anti-vacuity
      MS-3   optT("a")  NEW  WIN, two silent, not-in-drive       the `?` row
      MS-3b  optT("ab") NEW  WIN, two fired,  consumed 2         anti-vacuity
      MS-6 / MS-7 / SENT                                         unreachable

    ⚠ NO GENERATED BODY IS ASSERTED HERE, AND THE REASON IS THE TRUNCATION
    RATHER THAN A CHOICE. generateParse prints to cout, which is block buffered
    and flushed at exit; MS-5 crashes before that flush, so NO body reaches this
    file's capture at all -- optT's included, though optT is generated long
    before the crash. A row greping for it could only ever fail. The body is
    read in `tester`, which stops cleanly. Do not add a body row here until MS-5
    stops crashing.  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search GroupFields UnitTests Utilities list;
define
    optT isRule one-="a" two?-="b" ;
    repT isRule "a"+ ;
    msOptOld  code={ cerr "MS-1 optT OLD":;    traceParse("on"); optT("a");    traceParse("off"); cerr "MS-1-BACK":; };
    msOptOldB code={ cerr "MS-1b optTab OLD":; traceParse("on"); optT("ab");   traceParse("off"); cerr "MS-1b-BACK":; };
    msOptNew  code={ cerr "MS-3 optT NEW":;    traceParse("on"); optT("a");    traceParse("off"); cerr "MS-3-BACK":; };
    msOptNewB code={ cerr "MS-3b optTab NEW":; traceParse("on"); optT("ab");   traceParse("off"); cerr "MS-3b-BACK":; };
    msRepOld  code={ cerr "MS-5 repT OLD":;    traceParse("on"); repT("aaa");  traceParse("off"); cerr "MS-5-BACK":; };
    msRepNew  code={ cerr "MS-7 repT NEW":;    traceParse("on"); repT("aaa");  traceParse("off"); cerr "MS-7-BACK":; };
    ;
msOptOld();
msOptOldB();
cerr "MS-2 generate optT":;
parser(optT);
msOptNew();
msOptNewB();
msRepOld();
cerr "MS-6 generate repT":;
parser(repT);
msRepNew();
cerr "MODSEAM SENTINEL":;
stop();

/***************************************************************************
    MEASURED AND DELIBERATELY NOT INSTALLED -- two negative controls and one
    unexpressible drive. All three were run on this binary, 2026-09-22.

    1. THE H7 NEGATIVE CONTROL FOR THE `?`, AND IT CRASHES RATHER THAN LOSING.
       Drop the modifier -- ctlT isRule one-="a" two-="b" ; -- and drive
